				<h1>Crystal is now Nimbalyst: options for parallel Claude Code and Codex sessions</h1>
				<div class="post-meta">By Minh Ngo · Published <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time></div>
				<p class="lede">Crystal, the MIT app for running Claude Code and Codex sessions in parallel git worktrees, was deprecated in February 2026 and replaced by Nimbalyst. If you want another open-source option, Anywhere is a free MIT Mac app that runs Claude Code, Codex and eight more agents in separate worktrees, with status and diff review.</p>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Disclosure</span>The Anywhere team wrote this. Facts about other tools come from their own sites and repositories, checked on 18 November 2026.</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="what-happened-to-crystal">What happened to Crystal</h2>
				<p>The <a href="https://github.com/stravu/crystal">Crystal repository</a> now opens with "Crystal Is Now Nimbalyst". The README says Crystal is "deprecated and replaced by Nimbalyst", and dates it: "Deprecated: February 2026."</p>
				<p>As of 18 November 2026, the repository is still public, still MIT licensed, and not archived. The code stays readable and forkable. But if Crystal was part of your daily setup, you are now choosing what comes next.</p>

				<h2 id="what-you-need">What Crystal users need from a replacement</h2>
				<p>Crystal's core idea was simple: several Claude Code and Codex sessions at once, each in its own git worktree. Whatever you move to should keep that, plus the parts that make it workable day to day:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Several sessions running at once, on one repository, without touching each other's files.</li>
					<li>A worktree and branch per session, created for you.</li>
					<li>A clear signal when a session is done or waiting on you.</li>
					<li>A way to compare and review each session's changes before you merge them.</li>
					<li>Ideally, an open-source license, so you can keep the tool running even if its authors move on.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2 id="nimbalyst">Option 1: the official successor, Nimbalyst</h2>
				<p>Crystal's README names Nimbalyst as its replacement. If you want the path Crystal itself recommends, start from the <a href="https://github.com/stravu/crystal">Crystal repository</a>.</p>

				<h2 id="anywhere">Option 2: Anywhere</h2>
				<p>Anywhere is a desktop app for macOS 13 or later, with unsigned Linux and Windows builds. It is MIT licensed and costs nothing, with no account, no cloud tier, and no premium tier. For a Crystal user, the parts that matter:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><strong>Claude Code and Codex, side by side.</strong> Both run in a real terminal or as a chat, and report their status from either. Eight more agents run as a chat: Cursor, Grok Build, OpenCode, Pi, omp, fx, Hermes Agent, and Antigravity.</li>
					<li><strong>A worktree per session.</strong> Create it from a branch or a pull request. Setup commands in <code>.anywhere/config.json</code> or <code>.anywhere/setup.sh</code> run before the agent starts. See <a href="/docs/worktrees">worktrees</a>.</li>
					<li><strong>Status.</strong> Working, Needs you, Done, Failed, or Idle, in the sidebar, the Inbox, the Dock badge, and notifications.</li>
					<li><strong>Review.</strong> Diffs and commits beside the session, a compare against the base branch, and a pull request card. See <a href="/docs/review">review</a>.</li>
					<li><strong>Sessions that survive.</strong> A Go daemon, pocketd, owns the terminals, so agents keep running after the window closes.</li>
					<li><strong>Native.</strong> The window is Rust on GPUI, with no Electron or Tauri.</li>
				</ul>

				<h3>Mapping your Crystal workflow to Anywhere</h3>
				<div class="table-wrap">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr><th>What you did</th><th>Where it is in Anywhere</th></tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>Start a session in its own worktree</td><td><kbd>⌘⇧N</kbd>, or the <strong>+</strong> on a project's row. The new session page opens with <strong>Worktree</strong> picked</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Run Claude Code and Codex together</td><td>Pick the agent on the new session page. Sessions sit under the project and worktree, in tabs and splits</td></tr>
						<tr><td>See which session finished</td><td>Done in the sidebar and Inbox, a banner, the Dock badge</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Compare a session's work with main</td><td><strong>Changes</strong> › scope switch › pick the base branch</td></tr>
						<tr><td>Commit and open a pull request</td><td>The commit box, then <strong>Commit, push and create PR</strong></td></tr>
						<tr><td>Throw away a finished worktree</td><td>Right-click it › <strong>Delete worktree…</strong>, optionally with its branch</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
				</div>
				<p>Git worktrees you already made outside Anywhere show up in the project's menu as <strong>Import <var>n</var> worktrees</strong>, which adds them to the sidebar. For the full walkthrough, see <a href="/blog/run-multiple-claude-code-agents-in-parallel">How to run multiple Claude Code agents in parallel</a>.</p>

				<div class="cta">
					<p>Anywhere is MIT licensed, with no account and no paid tier.</p>
					<a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere">Star Anywhere on GitHub</a>
					<a class="button" href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere/releases/latest">Download for Mac</a>
				</div>

				<h2 id="other-open-source-options">Other open-source options</h2>
				<p>Crystal users care about open source, so here are the other MIT-licensed apps in this space, each in a line, in their own words where possible:</p>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="https://paradev.com/">Paradev</a> (formerly Superset): "The open-source workspace for Claude Code, Codex, and any coding agent." The desktop app is free; remote access, automations, mobile, and cloud are on paid plans.</li>
					<li><a href="https://www.trysynara.com/">Synara</a>: "a free, open-source, local-first workspace and control plane for coding agents", with Windows, macOS, and Linux builds and a paid Alto plan for remote and iPhone access.</li>
					<li><a href="https://orca.dev/">Orca</a>: "a desktop IDE for running multiple AI coding agents side by side", which works with any CLI agent, in chat or terminal, with a browser and a mobile companion.</li>
					<li><a href="https://zeron.sh/">Zeron</a>: "The agent workspace that can become anything." Native Rust on GPUI, with desktop builds for macOS, Windows, and Linux, mobile apps, and a headless server.</li>
					<li><a href="https://www.usemono.dev/">MonoCode</a>: "A GUI for your coding agents." Eleven agent CLIs, including Devin, with macOS, Windows, and Linux apps.</li>
				</ul>
				<p>The <a href="/blog/best-tools-run-multiple-coding-agents">roundup of seven tools</a> compares them in one table.</p>

				<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq">
					<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
					<h3>Is Crystal still maintained?</h3>
					<p>Crystal, the MIT app for running Claude Code and Codex sessions in parallel git worktrees, was deprecated in February 2026 and replaced by Nimbalyst. If you want another open-source option, Anywhere is a free MIT Mac app that runs Claude Code, Codex and eight more agents in separate worktrees, with status and diff review.</p>
					<h3>What replaced Crystal?</h3>
					<p>Nimbalyst. Crystal's README says Crystal is deprecated and replaced by Nimbalyst, and gives February 2026 as the deprecation date.</p>
					<h3>Is there an open-source Crystal alternative?</h3>
					<p>Yes. Anywhere is MIT licensed and free, with no account and no paid tier. Paradev, Synara, Orca, Zeron and MonoCode are also MIT-licensed apps for running coding agents in parallel. Crystal's own repository is still public under MIT.</p>
					<h3>Can I run Claude Code and Codex side by side like in Crystal?</h3>
					<p>Yes. In Anywhere, start each agent in its own worktree of the same project. Claude Code and Codex both run in a real terminal or as a chat, and both report Working, Needs you, Done, Failed or Idle from either.</p>
